## Decision: To solve these problems we propose to add the concept of an `OpTemplate` to the DSL. `OpTemplate`s are abstract
representations of an Op and are not themselves ops. They work similar to mixins in other languages, and can be used to
define an op like the following:

```kotlin
val ExampleTemplate = OpTemplate("Example") {
    Input(NUMERIC, "in")
    Input(NUMERIC, "addIn") { defaultValue = null }
    Input(NUMERIC, "mask") { defaultValue = null }
    Output(NUMERIC, "out")
    Doc(ANY, ALL) { """
      Does something
    """ }
}

fun MyNs() = Namespace("MyNs") {
    Op("OpOne", ExampleTemplate) {
        Arg(INT, "threshold") {
            defaultValue = 0
            description = """
              Threshold value that ...
            """
        }
        Doc(ANY, ALL) { """
          Does something for the purpose of the op one
        """ }
    }

    Op("OpTwo", ExampleTemplate) {
        Arg(INT, "anotherArg") {
            defaultValue = 100
            description = """
              Threshold value that ...
            """
        }
        Doc(ANY, ALL) { """
          Does something else for op two
        """ }
    }
}
```

The example above will create two ops: OpOne and OpTwo. They will have 4 Input fields, 1 Output field and a Doc field in
common. In addition, OpOne will have an arg of type INT named "threshold", and OpTwo an arg of type INT named
"anotherArg".

The behavior for each section type will be like the following:

- **Args**: Add all args to the existing ones
- **Attributes**: Add all attributes to the existing ones
- **Doc**: Combine all Doc sections with the current one, and remove duplicates based on [doc scope] and language

As you can see, the ExampleTemplate above already has a doc for the default scope `ALL`. For `OpOne` we extend the doc
with another doc also targeting the `ALL` scope, and the new doc will take precedence and the first one will be removed.

It is possible for an OpTemplate to inherit from one or more other OpTemplates. If an OpTemplate inherits from more than one
other OpTemplate, the sections will be processed based on their position in the `templates` list and from left to
right, where the last template will always take precedence over the others.

The following example will create an OpTemplate for pairwise ops. It already contains two default inputs, and a default
output.

```kotlin
val PairwiseTemplate = OpTemplate("Pairwise") {
  Input(NUMERIC, "x")
  Input(NUMERIC, "y")
  Input(NUMERIC, "mask") { defaultValue = null }
  Output(NUMERIC, "out")
}

fun MyNs() = Namespace("MyNs") {
  Op("MyPairwiseOp", PairwiseTemplate) {
    Arg(NUMERIC, "scalar")
    Doc(ANY, ALL) { """
      A very descriptive doc.
    """}
  }
}
```

## Decision: **Use JSONSchema to define and test the JSON structure of responses to GraphQL queries.**

### Schema files

Each type definition in GraphQL schema is mapped into a schema file in `spec/support/api_schemas`. For example, this will be in a directory structure that resembles the type structure in schema, for example, `spec/support/api_schemas/objects/location.json`.

An object schema in file can be:

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "required": [
    "id",
    "commonName",
    "stops"
  ],
  "properties": {
    "id": {
      "type": "string"
    },
    "commonName": {
      "type": "string"
    },
    "stops": {
      "type": "array"
    },
  },
  "additionalProperties": false
}
```

The top level properties for a GraphQL schema are `data`, `errors` and optionally, `extensions`. These can also be defined. For example:

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "required": [
    "data"
  ],
  "properties": {
    "data": {
      "$ref": "graphql_objects#/definitions/ObjectWithIdAndCommonName"
    },
  },
  "additionalProperties": false
}
```

Each schema is also indexed with a definition file `graphql_objects.json`, where each top level key is referenced by the `$ref` property for individual schema files, for example:

```json
{
  "definitions": {
    "ObjectWithIdAndCommonName": {
      "type": "object",
      "required": [
        "id",
        "commonName"
      ],
      "properties": {
        "id": {
          "$ref": "common#/definitions/Id"
        },
        "commonName": {
          "$ref": "common#/definitions/CommonName"
        }
      },
      "additionalProperties": false
    },
  }
}
```

Some GraphQL types (eg `ID` and `String`) will be used across a number of object schemas, so these can be grouped in a `common.json` definition file.

We can re-use JSONSchema `definitions`, if we have a GraphQL object type that has similar properties to other object types, as shown by `graphql_objects.json`.

We decided to use the `additionalProperties` and set it to false, so that we can easily detect unexpected keys and update schemas.


### Schema generators

In [this article](https://graphql-ruby.org/schema/introspection), GraphQL-Ruby suggests to generate a schema JSON from the schema. This could then be used by a separate generator to create our JSONSchema.

### Test framework

We decided to use the Ruby `jsonschema` library. It has not been updated for a few years, but it supports JSONSchema [v5 draft specification](http://json-schema.org/specification-links.html#draft-5), which at the time of writing, should be sufficient for the required functionality.

### Tooling

We wanted to add a rake task to lint all our schema files.

```rake
task :graphql_schema_lint do
  errors = []
  all_schemas.each do |schema|
    begin
      JSON::Schema.validate(schema, schema)
    rescue JSON::Schema::ValidationError => e
      errors << [schema['$id'], e.message]
    end
  end

  if errors.any?
    errors.each do |file, msg|
      warn "JSON Schema validation failed for #{file}:\n#{msg}"
    end

    fail 'JSON Schema validations failed.'
  end
end
```

## Decision: We are going to represent errors using the [Problem Details for HTTP APIs](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7807) standard.

The errors will be structured as JSON, for example:

    {
        "type": "https://example.org/docs/error#out-of-stock",
        "title": "Out of Stock",
        "detail": "We have 0 widgets left, but you requested 3"
    }

Validation errors will be represented as:

    {
        "type": "https://example.org/docs/error#validation",
        "title": "Validation Failed",
        "detail": "Some fields were invalid",
        "validationErrors": {
            "name": "is required",
            "quantity": "must be at least 1"
        }
    }

