## Decision: 1. Keep the Responsive component in our codebase to keep the library backwards compatible with our ecosystem and update `react-semantic-ui` to its latest version.
2. We decided to use React Hook's [`useState`](https://reactjs.org/docs/hooks-state.html) hook and [`window`](https://developer.mozilla.org/es/docs/Web/API/Window) object (which is a global object in the DOM representing a window containing a DOM document) to conditionally render components or change a component's properties based on their display.

We must remember that:
- This API isn't exactly like a responsive API and this is why we choose to keep a temporary responsive component in our codebase so the teams have time to adapt the UI for their apps.
- The window object is only available in the client side, if we try to use it in the server (e.g. while doing Server Side Rendering), it will cause an exception. However, we have a safe-check in our Responsive component (see decision 1) that it doesn't break our applications.

We encourage every team to use these APIs as best as they can, and we strongly recomend using them like this:

