## Decision: Instead of trying to hide the nature of HTTP, our client API will expose it through a more sophisticated method signature:

* Every client call will return an `HttpResponse` that models a real HTTP response: It has a status, headers and a body.
* The API will expose `HttpResponse` objects that have been parsed and deserialized for all responses where the deserialization schema is know to the client. This way both 2xx and 4xx/5xx responses can be parsed (e.g. for a custom error object) without any additional effort.
* If a response body is not known to the client API it will be exposed as an `InputStream` to avoid any overhead of having to read it to memory first.
* All error responses (any non 2xx status) will be flagged as failed by providing a `failureReason`
* Our API will expose a *single* exception for real connection or IO issues (`HttpClientConnectionException`) in line with traditional programming models. No other exceptions will be thrown for any HTTP response.

### Example

```java
final class SomeApiClient {

  public HttpResponse<SomeResource> createSomeResource(
      CreateSomeResourceRequest request
  ) { ... }

}
```

The resource creation client call could now return the following `HttpResponse` values:

* a successful 201 response with the `SomeResource` deserialized as the payload
* a successful 202 response with no payload. This would be modelled as `HttpResponse<Void>` and indicate that the request has been received and will be processed.
* a failed 400 response with `ErrorResponse` payload.

Each of these responses would be parsed and deserialized correctly without the client code having to implement any error handling using an exception/catch block model. Instead the client can handle each case correctly and independently of each other if needed using a switch/if-else approach like this:

```java
HttpResponse<SomeResource> response = client.createSomeResource(request);
if (response.isSuccess()) {
  ... do something with response.payload() ...
} else if (response.status() == BAD_REQUEST) {
  // we know that BAD_REQUEST has a specific ErrorResponse schema, so we can deserialize it
  ErrorResponse error = response.payload(ErrorResponse.class);
  ... do something with error ...
} else {
  // All other HTTP errors, including 5xx will fall into this bucket.
  // We don't have a known schema for the body, so we'll just log
  // the headers and status
  LOG.warn("unexpected failed response {} {}", response.status(), response.headers());
  response.payload().close(); // always close the input stream!
}
```

